  "teaching": {
    "task": "# Year 12 Physics M7 V06: Double-slit interference. Why do two slits produce regularly spaced bright and dark bands? (design)",
    "causalExplanation": "Remember superposition. When two waves meet, they add. If they arrive in phase, crest with crest, they build a bigger wave, and that's bright. If they arrive exactly half a cycle out, crest with trough, they cancel, and that's dark. That adding and cancelling is called interference. Pick a point on the screen. Light reaches it from both slits, but along two different paths. The waves leave the slits in step, so what matters is how much further one has travelled. That's the path difference. At the centre, both paths are equal, so the waves arrive in step. Bright. Move along until one path is a whole wavelength longer. The waves are back in step, so it's bright again. In between, where one path is half a wavelength longer, crest meets trough, and it's dark. So bright bands sit where the path difference is a whole number of wavelengths, and dark bands where it's a half more than a whole number. Light from both slits does reach the dark bands. It just cancels. This only works if the light from the two slits stays in step. The sources must be coherent, the same frequency, with a steady phase relationship. A laser does that. Thomas Young, who first did this experiment, made his own coherent light by sending it through one single slit first, so both slits were fed by the same wavefront. Two separate lamps won't work. Their phases jump around at random, so the bands shift billions of times a second and wash out into an even glow.",
