    "understandingCheck": "If light were a stream of particles, two slits would give two bright strips on the screen. Instead, you get a whole row of bright and dark bands, evenly spaced. Light from two slits, added together, makes some places dark. Make your prediction. Right at the centre of the screen, halfway between the two slits, is it bright or dark? It's bright. Let's see why. Your turn. Green light of wavelength five hundred and thirty-two nanometres passes through two slits nought point three zero millimetres apart, onto a screen one point eight metres away. Calculate the fringe spacing. Then state the new spacing if the slit separation is doubled. Pause here to answer. Last one. In a double-slit experiment with six hundred nanometre light, point P on the screen is the second dark band from the centre. Find the path difference to P, and explain why P is dark. Pause here to answer.",
    "curriculumScope": "# Year 12 Physics M7 V06: Double-slit interference. Why do two slits produce regularly spaced bright and dark bands? (design)\n\nStatus: Revision 3 (13 Nov 2026): visual-standard top-up (stage list, question-card pictures, givens strips, five-line notes, simulation engines named, root rules R1 to R4), visuals only. Narration unchanged. Needs a Codex error check before plates and voicing. Previously Revision 2 (7 Nov 2026): brief 191 findings resolved and visual upgrade to maths-visual-standard.md.\n\nNarration 6,253 characters, 16 scenes, about 539 s, counted by script with the build parser rules (total = sum of scene strings; see \"Narration length estimate\"). Opens on v05's final frame.\n\nScope: sixth video of Year 12 Physics Module 7 (The Nature of Light, Physics Stage 6, 2017 syllabus), inquiry question \"What evidence supports the classical wave model of light and what predictions can be made using this model?\". Templates as `phys-y12-m7-v01.md`; calculation layout as `math-a12-d10.md`. No pre-design pack exists; the pack work is done here. Site lesson read in full (6 Nov, read-only, through `work/brief168/module7-lesson03.sa.txt`, hash checked): orient and predict (two slits prediction, warm-up, intentions, vocabulary), Young's double slit (apparatus figure, path-difference conditions, x_n = nλL/d, Δx = λL/d, Stop and Check, MC), coherence and predictions (coherence, the equations box including d sin θ = nλ, white light, small-angle HSC tip, true/false), the worked calculation (632.8 nm, 0.25 mm, 2.4 m: 6.1 mm, 24.3 mm, 396 nm, white light), apply and consolidate (Activities 1 and 2), the two misconceptions (smaller d, dark fringes), \"Analyse 7 marks\", \"Assess 6 marks\" (white light, single reading, quoted d), independent practice (Q1, Q2, model answers), retrieve: `C:/Users/kygs/hscscience/subjects/physics/year12/module7/lesson03.sa.html`, SHA256 `8a4753307dca9df0579d6dbb777f6731903c7c1f70874e623534cb16390916c1` (recomputed 6 Nov